  "question": "Background / Context\n\nThis project focuses on developing an Access Control List (ACL) solution for Laravel versions 5 through 9. Known as Defender, this solution aims to enhance application security by providing permission management for single authentication setups. It is designed to integrate seamlessly with Laravel applications, ensuring user access is controlled without compromising the developer experience.\n\nObjectives\n\nThe primary objective is to implement a robust ACL system in Laravel applications that maintains a balance between security and usability. This system will ensure that users have access to necessary resources while safeguarding sensitive information.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Integration and implementation of the ACL within Laravel applications.\n- Administrators: Manage user roles and permissions to ensure appropriate access levels.\n- End Users: Benefit from secure access to resources based on roles and permissions.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n1. Role Management: \n   - Create, update, and delete roles.\n   - Assign unique names to roles to avoid duplication.\n\n2. Permission Management:\n   - Define, update, and delete permissions with both technical and readable names.\n   - Ensure permissions are uniquely identified by name.\n\n3. User Role Assignment:\n   - Associate users with specific roles, allowing them access to role-based resources.\n   - Efficient indexing for quick association lookup.\n\n4. Permission Assignment:\n   - Link permissions directly to roles and optionally set expiration.\n   - Grant specific permissions directly to users, with an optional expiration feature.\n\n5. Access Control:\n   - Use indexation and foreign key constraints to maintain data integrity and facilitate efficient access control across tables.\n   - Implement value-based and time-based permission checks.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Scalability: Ensure the system efficiently manages an increasing number of roles and permissions without performance degradation.\n- Security: Provide a secure method for managing access while protecting user data and system integrity.\n- Usability: Maintain simplicity in integration and operation to ensure developers can implement the ACL without additional complexity.",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0058\nCREATE TABLE `roles` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `roles_name_unique` (`name`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `permissions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `display_name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `permissions_name_unique` (`name`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `users_email_unique` (`email`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `role_user` (\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`role_id`,`user_id`),\n  KEY `role_user_user_id_index` (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `role_user_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `role_user_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `permission_role` (\n  `permission_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `expires_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`permission_id`,`role_id`),\n  KEY `permission_role_role_id_index` (`role_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `permission_role_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`permission_id`) REFERENCES `permissions` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `permission_role_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `permission_user` (\n  `permission_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `expires_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`permission_id`,`user_id`),\n  KEY `permission_user_user_id_index` (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `permission_user_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`permission_id`) REFERENCES `permissions` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `permission_user_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
